Falsifies `component_plan::a_plan_never_grants_a_right_the_declaration_did_not_ask_for`.

The defect: `Direction::rights` adds `GRANT` to every wiring. It reads as the
convenience it would be sold as, a component that can pass its rendezvous on to a
worker it spawns, and it is the one right this plan must never mint: a component
holding `GRANT` can hand its authority to anything it can reach, so the
supervisor's routing stops being the whole story of who can talk to whom. The
declaration has no way to ask for it, which is why the plan is where it has to be
refused.

Expected to fail: `assert!(p.caps()[i].1 & abi::rights::GRANT == 0)`, the third of
the four assertions in the loop, for every declaration in `SHAPES`. The two above
it, `p.caps()[i].1 == reqs.caps[i].direction.rights()` and the READ-or-WRITE
disjunction, are stated against `rights()` itself and so are blind to a change
inside it, which is worth knowing: the equality assertion follows the defect and
only the explicit `GRANT` check catches it. That is the same shape the
`derive_never_widens_rights` record found one crate over, a property stated
through the function it is testing, and here it is the reason a redundant-looking
assertion is not redundant.

diff --git a/crates/component_plan/src/lib.rs b/crates/component_plan/src/lib.rs
index b0a208f6..9c1739bd 100644
--- a/crates/component_plan/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/component_plan/src/lib.rs
@@ -198,8 +198,8 @@ impl Direction {
     /// the tree a component's rendezvous rights are decided.
     pub const fn rights(self) -> u64 {
         match self {
-            Direction::Serve => abi::rights::READ,
-            Direction::Use => abi::rights::WRITE,
+            Direction::Serve => abi::rights::READ | abi::rights::GRANT,
+            Direction::Use => abi::rights::WRITE | abi::rights::GRANT,
         }
     }
 }
